{
  "title": "Remove Duplicates from Sorted List II",
  "description": "> This is the classic *linked list* exercise. Because this judge works with plain values, the list is given to you as a `vector<int>` of the node values in order, and you return the node values of the resulting list in order.\n\nGiven the values of a **sorted** linked list, delete **every** value that appears more than once, keeping only the values that occur exactly one time. Return the resulting list, still sorted.\n\n### Examples\n\n| `values` | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| `[1, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 5]` | `[1, 2, 5]` |\n| `[1, 1, 1, 2, 3]` | `[2, 3]` |\n| `[1, 1]` | `[]` |\n\n### Constraints\n\n- `0 <= values.size() <= 300`\n- `-100 <= values[i] <= 100`\n- `values` is sorted in non-decreasing order.\n- Inputs are passed as function arguments and the answer is returned from the function. Do not read from standard input or print the result.\n",
  "reference": "vector<int> remove_all_duplicates(vector<int> values) {\n    vector<int> result;\n    size_t i = 0;\n    while (i < values.size()) {\n        size_t j = i;\n        while (j < values.size() && values[j] == values[i]) {\n            ++j;\n        }\n        if (j - i == 1) {\n            result.push_back(values[i]);\n        }\n        i = j;\n    }\n    return result;\n}\n",
  "template": "vector<int> remove_all_duplicates(vector<int> values) {\n    // Return only the values that appear exactly once, in order.\n    return {};\n}\n",
